\import{base-macros}
\author{kellenkanarios}
\title{Warp scheduling hides memory latency by switching among resident warps}
\tag{public}

\p{A GPU keeps many [warps](X9VZ) resident on each [[OGCS6]] and switches between them on every stall. When a warp issues a memory op that misses cache it is dead for hundreds of cycles — but with dozens of resident warps the scheduler always has a runnable one, so aggregate throughput stays high even though single-warp latency is awful. This is the [[G4ML]] design point cashed out on the GPU: spend silicon on resident warp state rather than on latency-reduction machinery. The switch is free because each SM's [register file](ETWN) is large enough to hold every resident warp's registers at once, so a context switch is just changing which warp the scheduler picks — not saving state to memory the way a CPU [context switch](OKCI) must.}

\p{Two things bound how well this works. [Occupancy](EPUD) — how many warps are resident — sets the ceiling on latency hiding: with too few warps the scheduler runs out of runnable candidates. And the scheduler itself must stay cheap enough to choose a warp every cycle, which it does with a [two-loop scoreboard check](7SGF), marshalling each issued instruction's register reads through an [operand collector](7QAW).}
